WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.520
Spoiler alert, but in case you didn't know,
the fourth episode of Game of Thrones' final

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.940
season was distressing in more ways than one.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.560
Viewers witnessed two deaths, a long-awaited
romance scene that was ruined just a few scenes

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.640
later, and a moment of abandonment that broke
a million hearts.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:18.940
Suffice to say, Thrones fans were none too
happy with episode 4, and things might get

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.740
even worse when they realize why the episode
was titled "The Last of the Starks."

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.010
Watching the latest Game of Thrones installment,
the answer seems obvious: The episode takes

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.680
its title from a line of dialogue Arya Stark
gives during a conversation with Sansa, Bran,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:32.180
and Jon.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.180
"We're family.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.480
The four of us.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.240
The last of the Starks."

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.100
Easy explanation, right?

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.520
Sure, but PureWow has offered up a deeper
significance to Arya's words and to the title

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.420
of episode 4.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.940
Arya, Sansa, and Bran do represent the last
living members of House Stark that fans know

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.040
of, but Jon isn't technically a Stark.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.280
Though his mother is Lyanna Stark, Jon belongs
to House Targaryen, having taken the last

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.200
name of his father Rhaegar Targaryen.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.160
And if Jon has children, according to the
rules of Westeros, they too would be Targaryens.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.970
With Jon out of the count for the last of
the living Starks, that leaves us with just

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.610
three true-born children of Ned and Catelyn
Stark who have survived the brutality of political

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.620
warfare over the course of the series.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.460
Some pretty big issues arise here.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.840
Bran has made it clear on several occasions
that not only is he not really a Stark anymore,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.880
it's arguable whether he's even still human.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.700
And in episode 4, he reiterates this in a
conversation with Tyrion, making it very clear

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.260
that he no longer considers himself the Stark
heir.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.160
"I'm not lord of Winterfell."

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.140
"You're the only surviving trueborn son of
Ned Stark."

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.500
"You don't want it."

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.240
"I don't really want anymore."

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.200
It seems unlikely at this point that Bran
will ever produce an heir.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.140
After all, he no longer wants anything.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.200
That leaves just Arya and Sansa as the last
of the Starks.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.060
But it also seems highly unlikely that Arya
will continue the Stark line either.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.600
Once, it seemed possible:

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.240
"I've never had a family."

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.100
"I can be your family."

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.200
But in this episode, Arya showed just how
much she has changed, gently but firmly rejecting

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.460
Gendry's marriage proposal.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.620
And any lady will be lucky to have you.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.140
But I'm not a lady.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:13.880
I never have been.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.480
Arya isn't about creating new life.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.960
She serves the god of death now.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.279
And in episode 4, she strongly hinted that
her days as a Stark, and possibly her days

00:02:22.279 --> 00:02:24.120
among the living, were coming to an end.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.160
"You're heading to King's Landing?"

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.580
"I have some unfinished business."

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:31.200
"Me too."

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.099
"I don't plan on coming back."

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:36.900
"Neither do I."

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.959
So could Sansa actually be the last of the
Starks?

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:43.590
She's already the Lady of Winterfell, and
that could remain true, as she might have

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.740
children who bear the Stark name rather than
her husband's name.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.140
Yes, children in Westeros usually take their
father's last name, but there are exceptions

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.120
when it comes to noble lines: Lady Lyanna
Mormont, for instance, inherited both her

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.360
family name and title from her mother, Lady
Maege Mormont.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.820
Even if Sansa were to die before having an
heir, it's unlikely that the long and famous

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.500
Stark line would be allowed to die out.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.860
After thousands of years of rule, the Starks
have intermarried with and are related to

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.080
all the other major noble families of the
north, most notably the Karstarks and the

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:13.580
Umbers.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.700
Of course, both of these lines were apparently
ended forever by the Night King's army, but

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:21.540
there are no doubt other distant cousins with
Stark blood who could take up the wolf banner

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.680
of Winterfell if needed.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:23.860
And it would be needed.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.599
After all, as Lyanna once famously said,

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.650
"We know no king but the King in the North,
whose name is Stark"

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.620
And that remains true even if the last Stark
isn't a king at all… but rather a Queen.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.420
"What if there's someone else?

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.480
Someone better?"

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.880
All hail the last of the Starks?

